{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Background research (moved out of the description, unchanged). The description keeps the decision, the confirmed mechanism, the scope, the safety property, and the acceptance criteria; this is the prior art and options analysis that produced the recommendation.\n\n## The problem (original framing)\n\nGerrit is the source of truth; GitHub is a read-only mirror. Gerrit's `Verified` label (CI per\npatchset) is the real merge gate. GitHub Actions ALSO runs on `main` post-merge as a safety net.\n\nWhen commits land close together the mirror's `main` runs get CANCELLED as superseded, so\nGitHub shows a long trail of red X's on a `main` that is actually healthy.\n\nOperator's framing: do NOT spend cycles validating every individual commit — Gerrit's\nper-patchset gate is the protection. Main CI matters as a safety net, but the value is\n\"is main healthy NOW\", not \"was every commit green\". Mirror what comparable projects do.\n\n## What comparable projects actually do (VERIFIED via GitHub API)\n\n| Mirror repo | Workflows | Check runs on latest main commit |\n|---|---|---|\n| golang/go | NONE (.github/workflows 404) | **0** |\n| openstack/nova | NONE | **0** |\n| LibreOffice/core | lockdown.yml only (issue-locking bot) | **0** |\n| aosp-mirror/platform_frameworks_base | - | **0** |\n| qt/qtbase | ninja-build.yml, `on: push` | outlier - see below |\n\n**Four of five major Gerrit-primary projects surface NO per-commit CI on the GitHub mirror at\nall.** The mirror is a code mirror, full stop. Qt is the exception and its answer is to make the\njob cheap enough that cancellation never matters (\"finish as fast as possible\", system libs,\nccache) rather than to report per-commit truth.\n\nWhere their real post-merge signal lives:\n- **Go** - post-submit on LUCI (ci.chromium.org/p/golang), dashboard at build.golang.org. Never GitHub.\n- **OpenStack/Zuul** - the closest precedent to what the operator wants, and it is a FIRST-CLASS\n  FEATURE, not a workaround. Zuul's `post` pipeline uses the **supercedent** manager, which\n  collapses queued items per project-ref: \"If more than one additional item is enqueued for the\n  project-ref, previously enqueued items which have not started processing are removed.\" That is\n  literally \"test main's current state, not every commit\". Separate `periodic` (daily 02:00 UTC),\n  `periodic-weekly`, `periodic-stable` pipelines carry the health-over-time signal.\n- **Kubernetes Prow/Tide** - batches PRs, tests the batch, merges together. Tide's docs describe\n  NO post-merge branch testing; the model is entirely pre-merge.\n- **Chromium/ChromeOS** - post-submit builders trigger every **7-10 hours**, deliberately covering\n  many commits at once, with sheriffs watching. This is the direct answer to the bisect question.\n\n## GitHub Actions mechanics (VERIFIED against docs)\n\n- **Cancelled == red X cannot be fixed natively.** GitHub staff acknowledged it Dec 2021; still\n  open in 2026 (community discussion 8336).\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` alone does NOT fix it.** With the default `queue: single`, an\n  existing PENDING run in the group is cancelled and replaced - you still get a red X, just on a\n  different run.\n- **A workflow cannot decline to report.** Actions creates CHECK RUNS intrinsically; the\n  `statuses:` permission governs only commit statuses pushed via API, so `statuses: none` does\n  nothing.\n- **The only \"hide\" path is deleting runs after the fact** (MercuryTechnologies/delete-cancelled-runs).\n- **GitHub merge queue is structurally inapplicable** - `merge_group` is driven by PRs entering a\n  queue; with Gerrit owning the merge decision there are no PRs and `main` advances by mirror push.\n\n## Options (option 3 was chosen)\n\n1. **Drop mirror CI entirely** (Go / OpenStack / LibreOffice / AOSP model). Zero red X's, zero\n   cycles. Cost: loses the safety net unless a Gerrit-side post-merge job replaces it.\n2. **Collapse instead of cancel** (Zuul supercedent semantics). GitHub cannot express this\n   natively; closest is today's behavior plus run deletion. Hack by its own author's admission.\n3. **Schedule-only main health run** (`on: schedule`, drop the `push` trigger for `main`).\n   The ONLY option that removes cancellations by construction rather than hiding them. Surfaces\n   as a workflow badge / Actions tab, not a per-commit mark - which matches the stated intent.\n   Cost: attribution.  **← CHOSEN, at a 6-hour cadence.**\n4. **Keep per-commit but make it cheap** (Qt model). Preserves attribution; costs cycles\n   proportional to commit rate.\n\n**The attribution cost, and how these projects pay it:** Chromium proves batching is survivable -\npost-submit every 7-10 hours, accepting a multi-commit regression range, closed by AUTOMATED\nbisection (Findit: median 14 min for compile failures, 24 min for swarmed gtest) plus a HUMAN on\nsheriff rotation plus tree-closure to stop the bleeding. OpenStack pays it differently: the gate\npipeline is strong enough that post-merge breakage is rare, and `post` jobs are largely artifact\npromotion rather than validation.\n\nBecause Gerrit's `Verified` gate already tests every patchset, a red scheduled run means one of:\na semantic conflict between two independently-verified changes, environmental/dependency drift,\nor a flake. The commit window between last-green and red is small enough that\n`git bisect run <the same CI script>` resolves it in log2(n) steps - typically 3-5 builds.\n\n## What NOT to do, and why\n\n- **delete-cancelled-runs**: makes the timeline LOOK right while destroying the record, and fails\n  open - a genuine failure racing a delete is indistinguishable.\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` as a one-liner**: does not work (see mechanics above).\n- **GitHub merge queue**: structurally inapplicable.\n- **`queue: max`**: does produce all-green per-commit, but spends MORE cycles than today and\n  directly contradicts the stated intent."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785951806544172001,"uuid":"84de10a8-a2c2-4e1e-94d1-03651c84e0c1"}